A VoidOS image contains a large body of independently licensed software,
including the Linux kernel, the GNU C Library, GNU Bash and coreutils, GTK,
wlroots, labwc, Tor and many others. That software is licensed by its own
authors under its own terms, which include the GNU General Public License, the
GNU Lesser General Public License, and various permissive licenses.

For the avoidance of doubt:

  (a) This License does not modify, supersede, or add any condition to the
      license of any third-party component.
  (b) Nothing in Section 3 restricts any right granted to you by the license of
      a third-party component. Where a third-party license permits you to copy,
      modify or redistribute that component, you may do so, and Section 3 does
      not apply to that act.
  (c) If any term of this License conflicts with the license of a third-party
      component, the third-party license prevails as to that component.
  (d) Copyleft-licensed components carry an obligation to provide corresponding
      source. See SOURCE.md for how to obtain it.

This section exists because imposing further restrictions on copyleft software
would violate its license. It is not a formality.


6. NAME AND BRANDING

"VoidOS", the VoidOS logo and the VoidOS visual identity are used as unregistered
marks of the VoidOS Project. No trademark registration is claimed, and nothing
here asserts a registered right.

Instead, the following is a CONDITION OF THIS LICENSE. Your permission under
Section 2 to use the VoidOS Original Works depends on observing it:

  (a) You may use the name nominatively — to state accurately that something
      runs on, is compatible with, is derived from, or interoperates with
      VoidOS. Saying "built on VoidOS" or "tested on VoidOS" is fine.

  (b) You may not name a modified system "VoidOS", use the VoidOS logo or visual
      identity for it, or present it as being VoidOS or as approved, verified or
      published by the VoidOS Project.

Condition (b) rests on this License and on your need for permission to use the
VoidOS Original Works — not on trademark law. If you build a system using only
third-party components under their own licenses (Section 5), this License does
not govern that system; but you would also not be shipping the VoidOS desktop,
theme or applications, which are what make an image VoidOS.


